#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171718 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "308a8c68-628a-522f-9545-17a7165aefdf" :knowledge-point/title "Finite Vs. Infinite Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "23214c12-0d2f-54f1-8d27-d6a70406ff58" :question/math-academy-id "e-13173" :question/problem "Do the sets below have the same cardinality, and why?\n\n$$\nA=\\{ 4n \\mid \\: n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\}, \\qquad B=\\{ 2, 4,  \\ldots, 20 \\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinalities of two sets cannot be equal if one set is finite and the other is infinite.\n\nWith that in mind, notice that \n\n- $A=\\{ 0, \\pm 4, \\pm 8, \\ldots \\}$ is infinite, while\n\n- $B$ is finite with  $|B| = 10.$\n\nTherefore, $|A| \\neq |B|.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude the following:\n\nSince $A$ is infinite and $B$ is finite, we have that $|A| \\neq |B|$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171719 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13fe2373-2094-5193-8e37-541d3a27e5e1" :knowledge-point/title "Using Arithmetic Sequences to Construct a Bijection"}] :question/id #uuid "1f394047-302d-561b-bae6-b88a881d68f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13180" :question/problem "Do the following sets have the same cardinality? If so, why?\n\n$$\nA= \\{ -7, -2, 3, 8, \\ldots  \\}, \\qquad B = \\mathbb{N}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $A$ form an (infinite) arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =5 \\\\  & =-2-(-7) \\\\  & =3-(-2) \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-7+(n-1)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-7+5n-5 \\\\  & =5n-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{5n-12 \\mid \\:  n \\in \\mathbb{N}  \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f: B \\to A$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $B$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $\\cdots$ | $n$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $-7$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $8$ | $\\cdots$ | $5n-12$ | $\\cdots$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n \\in B,$ we have $f(n) = 5n-12 \\in A.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n) \\neq f(m)$ whenever $n \\neq m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $A$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $B$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A| = |B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171744 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0445833-499f-577f-8ca7-265f72d24b77" :knowledge-point/title "Using Geometric Sequences to Construct a Bijection"}] :question/id #uuid "5f47fd84-fda1-5eba-b32e-53b07a391b3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14748" :question/problem "Do the following sets have the same cardinality? If so, why?\n\n$$\nA = \\mathbb{N}, \\qquad B = \\left\\{1,\\, \\dfrac12,\\,  \\dfrac14,\\,  \\dfrac18,\\,  \\ldots \\right \\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $B$ form an (infinite) geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=\\dfrac12{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =1\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $B$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nB = \\left\\{  \\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1} \\,  \\Bigg |\\:  n \\in \\mathbb{N}  \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f: A \\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $A$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $\\cdots$ | $n$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac18$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1}$ | $\\cdots$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n \\in A,$ we have $f(n) =   \\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1} \\in B.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n) \\neq f(m)$ whenever $n \\neq m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $B$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $A$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A| = |B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0445833-499f-577f-8ca7-265f72d24b77" :knowledge-point/title "Using Geometric Sequences to Construct a Bijection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ab8a727-abd6-5eee-91b1-5176578c7a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "surjective" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414261} {:answer/id #uuid "996e8f12-3b45-562f-a82b-60cba4333d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increasing" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414307} {:answer/id #uuid "eef5fb9c-5d39-552a-8b38-51c6a497a262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ab8a727-abd6-5eee-91b1-5176578c7a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "surjective" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414261} :answer-field/id #uuid "226a71c8-8ee0-5ede-b4fb-4602d14e2399" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414375} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26e5060e-a47f-5148-b58b-7f347b027a64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cdot {3}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414251} {:answer/id #uuid "730bdc07-19a7-515b-a282-86cf593fa9fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414286} {:answer/id #uuid "cffe121f-d0d3-573d-8166-b80e543428bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414336} {:answer/id #uuid "d8380d51-6839-56f7-aca1-b953369cebd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414340} {:answer/id #uuid "db7c03a5-d525-5ab5-89bb-e3868d5e36e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3n+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8380d51-6839-56f7-aca1-b953369cebd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414340} :answer-field/id #uuid "af555a01-5a46-56eb-990a-f86aa1f4392b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414397} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "326dcf9c-7c4f-56da-87bf-d4b8c35be676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|A|\\ne |B|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414258} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3c1429-9fec-53d1-8796-cb7d78667725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|A|=|B|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe3c1429-9fec-53d1-8796-cb7d78667725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|A|=|B|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414366} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5e830d8-4775-54a6-8f79-f32d4be3ba87" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414399} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ddee33-7744-5ed1-9e92-d7aa8dfc6ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "injective" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414231} {:answer/id #uuid "12e35f19-ae43-50ea-84ae-332c8e379c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "positive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414237} {:answer/id #uuid "e237efc1-efd8-5f4f-94ad-18272b4fb940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03ddee33-7744-5ed1-9e92-d7aa8dfc6ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "injective" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414231} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7976db9-2db5-5508-b5c5-470334703fc9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5b3d3fb-177d-5a6c-bcc7-9d4791b3f5d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-181594" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\{3,6,12,24,…\\},\\,B=N\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ (shown above):\n\nSince the function $f:B\\to A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf:n\\to {{field-1}}\n$$\n\nis both {{field-2}} and {{field-3}}, we have that {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $A$ form an (infinite) geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nA=\\{3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}∣\\,n\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f:B\\to A$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $B$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $3$ | $6$ | $12$ | $24$ | $⋯$ | $3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in B,$ we have $f(n)=3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}\\in A.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $A$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $B$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414417 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "308a8c68-628a-522f-9545-17a7165aefdf" :knowledge-point/title "Finite Vs. Infinite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "411917dc-6c93-55d0-978d-0767736c6121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414263} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf6f434-9fed-5970-b8e2-d17962421f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414269} {:answer/id #uuid "6e02cd42-42d0-5705-a005-75524f936d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414282} {:answer/id #uuid "ab558918-6e8c-59eb-9a7e-4397683388cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414317} {:answer/id #uuid "afae2ee4-4ec6-5251-afbc-9e17da7a7304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cf6f434-9fed-5970-b8e2-d17962421f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414269} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0ec5345-dc55-53da-93f5-32f3809241ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1261accc-9253-5b2b-9450-d1cf9dd3a558" :question/math-academy-id "q-181624" :question/problem "$$\nX=\\{a,b,2,4\\},\\,Y=\\{n\\in N∣\\,1\\le n<5\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the sets $X$ and $Y$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $|X|=4$\n\nII. $|Y|=5$\n\nIII. $|X|=|Y|$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that finite sets have the same cardinality if and only if they have the same number of elements.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $X$ is a finite set and $|X|=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $Y=\\{1,2,3,4\\}$ is a finite set and $|Y|=4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $X$ and $Y$ are finite sets and  $|X|=|Y|=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "308a8c68-628a-522f-9545-17a7165aefdf" :knowledge-point/title "Finite Vs. Infinite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16800456-6759-58c6-a585-7041bbe5d62e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414240} {:answer/id #uuid "a15fa94e-2fd4-55b5-8a43-b2bdf463ba84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414309} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a3cd4d-640f-57c2-a20a-46482aeaf94b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414324} {:answer/id #uuid "d96d5e4e-6751-5dec-b3c1-3719c09df64e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414342} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b46f19-d690-587d-9747-1e56dd29c668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16800456-6759-58c6-a585-7041bbe5d62e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414240} :answer-field/id #uuid "366327fe-3c8d-5196-b31e-017b57838927" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c2743b2-72ab-5576-b421-aa5dcedd6c11" :question/math-academy-id "q-181626" :question/problem "$$\nS=\\{1,1,2,3,\\{4,5\\}\\},\\,T=\\{n\\in N∣\\,2\\le n\\le 7\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the sets $S$ and $T$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $|S|=4$\n\nII. $|T|=5$\n\nIII. $|S|=|T|$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that finite sets have the same cardinality if and only if they have the same number of elements.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,  $S$ is a finite set and $|S|=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that $T=\\{2,3,4,5,6,7\\}$ is a finite set and $|T|=6.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $4\\ne 6,$ we conclude that $|S|\\ne |T|.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/