answer/id #uuid "144f1a7e-f5ee-5b2b-9875-8c4c251dd99a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cdot {3}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414238} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0f7338-ebdf-5402-9f3b-e915edca15ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{2}^{n}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414294} {:answer/id #uuid "96de4f09-9c29-5916-bca8-81e8413b1e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2n+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414304} {:answer/id #uuid "cdaf4c08-1b80-54b9-9e35-f6320fa2282f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{n}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414335} {:answer/id #uuid "e91c11d5-ad90-5a02-837f-30cf665abf94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4n+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96de4f09-9c29-5916-bca8-81e8413b1e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2n+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414304} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff14ff76-eafd-553a-b96d-1e02876d3683" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e8f5aa7-7ccd-5ed3-b543-c8ead40f433d" :question/math-academy-id "q-181681" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\{2,0,-2,-4,…\\},\\,B=N\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ (shown above):\n\nSince the function defined for each $n\\in N$ as\n\n$$\nf:n\\to {{field-1}}\n$$\n\nis a {{field-2}} of $B$ onto $A,$ we have that {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $A$ form an (infinite) arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =-2 \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2-0 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =2+(n-1)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =2-2n+2 \\\\  & =-2n+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nA=\\{-2n+4∣\\,n\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f:B\\to A$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $B$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $2$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-4$ | $⋯$ | $-2n+4$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in B,$ we have $f(n)=-2n+4\\in A.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $A$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $B$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13fe2373-2094-5193-8e37-541d3a27e5e1" :knowledge-point/title "Using Arithmetic Sequences to Construct a Bijection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "109421d6-88fa-52a8-974a-f85b7d91314e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{n}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414234} {:answer/id #uuid "16d4a2f9-e593-5566-822b-84853d2f0ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414241} {:answer/id #uuid "26796553-8f80-51bc-9a2e-35ca8a8cb416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{n}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414250} {:answer/id #uuid "61731695-4507-53d3-8c18-99d25efe6709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/