186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "positive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67727631-35bc-5f82-8d3a-de9e0aac8ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "injective" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414279} :answer-field/id #uuid "84b7562f-be27-5aff-94f5-a3eb063ffd99" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414388} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97b00b3e-da29-54ff-83d5-338841b50f95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414305} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b7a2a4-fb12-5246-86e1-7104292ea9ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increasing" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414313} {:answer/id #uuid "ab09ceb5-4998-5c22-be12-7d14477b6cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "surjective" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab09ceb5-4998-5c22-be12-7d14477b6cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "surjective" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414316} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab4a98f8-d784-53be-8554-ae2490cbe22d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e32b700f-379c-5d3a-b0db-4859531b5dcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-181743" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\{5,7,9,11,…\\},\\,B=N\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ (shown above):\n\nSince the function $f:B\\to A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf:n\\to {{field-1}}\n$$\n\nis both {{field-2}} and {{field-3}}, we have that {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $A$ form an (infinite) arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =2 \\\\  & =7-5 \\\\  & =9-7 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =5+(n-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5+2n-2 \\\\  & =2n+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nA=\\{2n+3∣\\,n\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f:B\\to A$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $B$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ | $11$ | $⋯$ | $2n+3$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in B,$ we have $f(n)=2n+3\\in A.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $A$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $B$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414428 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0445833-499f-577f-8ca7-265f72d24b77" :knowledge-point/title "Using Geometric Sequences to Construct a Bijection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c029dc4-9cdd-5258-b330-6f2a04deee63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414281} {:answer/id #uuid "705db018-fef4-583d-8f8c-91419b7e2819" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414285} {:answer/id #uuid "782f6fc0-88b4-5ed1-8e6b-b224a9823a35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.ty