f 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "injection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414289} {:answer/id #uuid "f38cff26-c65d-5cd1-901d-09a88c0b564f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "surjection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e737053-c0b4-5eef-94f3-1ec52eb51a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "bijection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414246} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee38ff5a-4d45-5456-b2ba-19cd9120e6cb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec2d5fbd-6cc2-569f-94cc-7ef69e7f43a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-181749" :question/problem "$$\nA=N,\\,B=\\{2,6,18,54,…\\}\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ above:\n\nSince the function defined for each $n\\in N$ as\n\n$$\nf:n\\to {{field-1}}\n$$\n\nis a {{field-2}} of $A$ onto $B,$ we have that {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $B$ form an (infinite) geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =3 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{6} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =2\\cdot {3}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $B$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nB=\\{2\\cdot {3}^{n-1}∣\\,n\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f:A\\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $A$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B$ | $2$ | $6$ | $18$ | $54$ | $⋯$ | $2\\cdot {3}^{n-1}$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in A,$ we have $f(n)=2\\cdot {3}^{n-1}\\in B.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $B$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $A$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0445833-499f-577f-8ca7-265f72d24b77" :knowledge-point/title "Using Geometric Sequences to Construct a Bijection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7527eec2-e171-5668-82cf-42036aab6253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414288} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9cfe8a-8e63-5a8b-9c72-a2f86ba721f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increasing" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414334} {:answer/id #uuid "d87ff7e0-7698-5b3c-b75a-548391b1d46c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "surjective" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d87ff7e0-7698-5b3c-b75a-548391b1d46c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "surjective" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414341} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b7ae9b9-5aac-5ea8-b929-98bbeac7e2a1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414382} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae1a040d-2d6d-590d-85ac-bb62b6fecc64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|A|=|B|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414318} {:answer/id #uuid "eee3a5e6-fd55-599e-89da-080807eb602c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/i