n\nNow, consider the function $f:A\\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $A$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B$ | $2$ | $10$ | $50$ | $250$ | $⋯$ | $2\\cdot {5}^{n-1}$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in A,$ we have $f(n)=2\\cdot {5}^{n-1}\\in B.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $B$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $A$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "308a8c68-628a-522f-9545-17a7165aefdf" :knowledge-point/title "Finite Vs. Infinite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bcc32c3-7dac-5043-8989-0510498a8612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|U|\\ne |V|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414254} {:answer/id #uuid "9239ecfb-0eca-5feb-ad3c-12cda3308896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|U|=|V|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bcc32c3-7dac-5043-8989-0510498a8612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|U|\\ne |V|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414254} :answer-field/id #uuid "34432844-0987-51f3-9987-b6fc82920753" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414379} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "793988a6-78c7-575e-afec-c31a26848491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "infinite" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414292} {:answer/id #uuid "e42350b7-6ba4-5b3b-b69b-21e3d07d3bbe" :answer/type {:db/id #e