$B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $B$ form an (infinite) arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =-2 \\\\  & =1-3 \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted ${b}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =3+(n-1)(-2) \\\\  & =3-2n+2 \\\\  & =5-2n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $B$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nB=\\{5-2n∣\\,n\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f:A\\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $A$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $⋯$ | $5-2n$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in A,$ we have $f(n)=5-2n\\in B.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $B$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $A$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13fe2373-2094-5193-8e37-541d3a27e5e1" :knowledge-point/title "Using Arithmetic Sequences to Construct a Bijection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ad6fb88-5156-5ca7-a2bc-92460ffef6b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|A|\\ne |B|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414253} {:answer/id #uuid "37f43ebd-0002-534a-b66a-2daa040139df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|A|=|B|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37f43ebd-0002-534a-b66a-2daa040139df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|A|=