4311} :answer-field/id #uuid "be0b2b0a-b1be-5c67-a5f6-cae22d0082d9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414402} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03306edf-541c-55bb-bdc4-0c703c1cce39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|A|\\ne |B|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414230} {:answer/id #uuid "ee6aeca2-f6d4-5e51-a143-cdabedf162f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|A|=|B|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee6aeca2-f6d4-5e51-a143-cdabedf162f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|A|=|B|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414355} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebba6751-55d0-5779-b8c6-94ff7e2af4f7" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f930f3c4-3dbb-5481-a48e-987b268b6aa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-216560" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\{27,9,3,1,…\\},\\,B=N\n$$\n\nComplete the following reasoning about the sets $A$ and $B$ (shown above):\n\nSince the function $f:B\\to A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf:n\\to {{field-1}}\n$$\n\nis both {{field-2}} and {{field-3}}, we have that {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $A$ form an (infinite) geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=\\frac{1}{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{27} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{9} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted ${a}_{n},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =27{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nA=\\{27{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1}\\,|\\,n\\in N\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f:B\\to A$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $B$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $⋯$ | $n$ | $⋯$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $27$ | $9$ | $3$ | $1$ | $⋯$ | $27{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1}$ | $⋯$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n\\in B,$ we have $f(n)=27{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1}\\in A.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n)\\ne f(m)$ whenever $n\\ne m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $A$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $B$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A|=|B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality."}]}
